# Repo code work: read CONTRIBUTING.md, lint before pushing

At the **start of every session** that changes **this repository's code or docs** (fixes, features, tests, PRs), read [`CONTRIBUTING.md`](../../CONTRIBUTING.md) with the Read tool and follow it. Do not rely on a memory of it from a prior turn — re-read when starting new work that ships a PR or user-facing change.

Pay special attention to:

1. One concern per PR; conventional-commit titles (`feat` `fix` `chore` `docs` `test` `refactor` `perf` `security` `release` — nothing else)
2. Docs + CHANGELOG for every user-visible change
3. New CLI subcommands / config keys / lint rules → matching row in `docs/reference/*.md` (CI enforces CLI coverage)
4. No new runtime deps — stdlib plus `markdown` only
5. No real session data; no personal vault paths, OS usernames, hostnames, or vault roots in PRs, commits, issue comments, or CHANGELOG — use placeholders (`/home/USER/…`, `<vault>`, `<user>`). Ready-to-paste smoke/live-vault shell blocks in operator chat may use real local paths when known; see CONTRIBUTING Privacy rules.
6. Link issues with `Closes #N` only when the PR fully completes the issue; otherwise `Relevant to #N`. Completeness is judged against what the issue itself owns — work it hands to another issue does not count
7. **No GitHub issue numbers in new test filenames** — use a stable feature/module slug (`tests/test_unified_document_pages_acceptance.py`, not `tests/test_305_….py`). Link the issue or AWOS spec in the module docstring / `# @spec:` comments. Existing `test_<digits>_…` files are legacy; do not add new ones (full rule: [`docs/CODING_STANDARDS.md`](../../docs/CODING_STANDARDS.md))
8. Before adding a test, check `tests/` for existing coverage — extend it rather than duplicating; a test for a new rule must fail when that rule is removed. Agent detail: [`docs/CODING_STANDARDS.md`](../../docs/CODING_STANDARDS.md); commands and 87% floor: CONTRIBUTING *Testing*

## Markdown formatting

**Never hard-wrap prose at a fixed column.** One paragraph is one line, however long. Line width is the renderer's job, not the file's.

## Lint and test before pushing

Run both from the repo root before any push:

```bash
ruff check llmwiki tests scripts   # lint
python3 -m pytest tests/ -q        # tests
```

A committed `pre-push` hook in `.githooks/` lints the Python files in your push and rejects it on violations. `./setup.sh` wires it; enable it by hand with `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`. Prefer `ruff check --fix --select …` for safe rule families — never bare `ruff check --fix` (it can delete deliberate re-exports). `git push --no-verify` bypasses the hook if you must — say why in the PR.

## After you push — wait for CI

Local green is not enough. After every `git push` that creates or updates a PR, **wait for GitHub Actions on that head SHA**, report green/red to the user, and if anything failed: read logs (`gh run view <id> --log-failed`), fix, push, wait again. Prefer `gh pr checks <n> --watch`. Do not call the PR ready while required checks are pending (unless the user says not to wait). Full detail: CONTRIBUTING *After you push*.

## PR checklist boxes

When writing or updating a PR body, include only the Pre-merge checks that apply to *this* PR — not a fixed dump of the template. Mark `- [x]` for what you verified this session (or N/A with a one-line waiver). Leave `- [ ]` only for what you cannot verify yourself. When new evidence arrives (e.g. CI goes green), update the PR body (`gh pr edit`).

## Do not confuse with AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md

[`AGENTS.md`](../../AGENTS.md) and [`CLAUDE.md`](../../CLAUDE.md) in this repo are **vault wiki schemas** (how agents maintain `raw/` → `wiki/` → `site/` for a user's knowledge base). They are **not** contribution guides for developing llmwiki itself. Do not put repo/PR/process rules into them.
